<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lietuvininkai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prussian Lithuanians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lietuvininkai, or Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, were Lithuanian-speaking Lutherans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> Minor, the north-eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. Their region produced the first Lithuanian printed book in 1547 and the poet Kristijonas Donelaitis, and Lithuanian books banned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> were printed there. Many were bilingual and loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and after 1945 most of those remaining were expelled or fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lietuvininkai.jpg" alt="Lietuvininkai" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zscout370 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lithuania_Minor.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The land between the Alle and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>, once home to Old Prussian, Skalvian and Curonian tribes, was left almost empty by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-crusade">Prussian Crusade</a> and the wars between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. Once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno">Treaty of Melno</a> fixed the border in 1422, better conditions in the Order's state drew many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> to settle there. After 1525 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a> turned Protestant, and by his will services for his Lithuanian subjects were held in their language. Educated Protestant émigrés such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martynas-mazvydas">Martynas Mažvydas</a> and Abraomas Kulvietis became early professors at Königsberg University, founded in 1544.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The plague of 1700-1721 killed 53 percent of the people of Lithuania Minor, and more than 90 percent of the dead were Prussian Lithuanians; Frederick II brought in settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, the Palatinate and Nassau to replace them. Most Prussian Lithuanians took little interest in the Lithuanian national revival of the late 19th century, though German was made compulsory in schools after 1871 and petitions of 1879 and 1896 asked for Lithuanian to return. When the Klaipėda Region was joined to Lithuania after 1923, many felt threatened by its Lithuanization policy and turned to German parties, and in the 1925 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> 34,337 people called themselves Memellanders.</p><p>The Nazis replaced Lithuanian place names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> with German ones in 1938, though Lithuanian services continued in Tilsit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragnit">Ragnit</a> until the evacuation of late 1944. Many refugees died under Soviet strafing or in the cold, and those who stayed suffered mass murder, rape and looting. The Soviet authorities treated them as Germans: the Klaipėda Region had about 20,000 inhabitants in 1945 against 152,800 in 1939, about 3,500 people were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> in 1951, and after 1958 most of those left emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Loyalty to the German state, strong faith and the mother tongue were the three marks of their identity, and they called the Catholic Lithuanians across the border <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a>. Until the mid-19th century most were villagers; from the mid-18th century most could read, and about 40 percent belonged to Pietist fellowships living by ascetic rules. Most were bilingual, speaking a Low Prussian dialect of Low German beside a Lithuanian rich in German loanwords and printed in Gothic type with a spelling in the German style. Many surnames are patronymics ending in -eit or -at, such as Abromeit and Szameitat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prussian Lithuanians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most inhabitants were German-speaking or bilingual <b>Lietuvininkai</b>, and conflicts between German parties and the Lithuanian governor were constant.</blockquote><a href="/state/klaipeda-region">Klaipėda Region</a></li></ul></section>
